  "sources": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "claim": "Air molecules scatter light by Rayleigh scattering, which depends inversely on wavelength to the fourth power, so short wavelengths are scattered far more than long ones.",
      "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~lecturedemonstrations/Composer/Pages/84.45.html",
      "title": "84.45 -- Sunset",
      "quote": "This type of scattering, in which the scattering centers are much smaller than the wavelength of the light, is known as *Rayleigh scattering*, and it ..."
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "claim": "Blue light is scattered so much more than red that it fills the daytime sky with blue.",
      "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~lecturedemonstrations/Composer/Pages/84.45.html",
      "title": "84.45 -- Sunset",
      "quote": "This is why the sky is blue; blue light is scattered so much more greatly than red light that it gives the sky its blue color, and it leaves the light..."
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "claim": "Some violet light is absorbed by the upper atmosphere, and our eyes are not as sensitive to violet as they are to blue.",
      "url": "https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/about/k-12-education/atmosphere/why-the-sky-blue",
      "title": "NOAA NESDIS: Why is the sky blue?",
      "quote": "Also, our eyes are not as sensitive to violet as they are to blue."
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "claim": "At sunset, sunlight takes a much longer path through the atmosphere, so most of the blue light and other wavelengths shorter than red are scattered out, leaving mostly red light.",
      "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~lecturedemonstrations/Composer/Pages/84.45.html",
      "title": "84.45 -- Sunset",
      "quote": "At sunset, light from the sun takes a significantly longer path than during the day, so that most of the blue light, and light of other wavelengths sh..."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "claim": "The air mass (the path through the atmosphere relative to straight up) is exactly one at the zenith and about 38 at the horizon, at sea level.",
      "url": "https://aty.sdsu.edu/explain/extinction/airmass/intro.html",
      "title": "Andrew T. Young, San Diego State University: Atmospheric extinction, air mass",
      "quote": "at the horizon, it's about 38, at sea level."
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "claim": "NOAA's Storm Prediction Center notes that sunrises and sunsets are red because the daytime sky is blue, since the same scattering removes blue from the direct beam.",
      "url": "https://www.spc.noaa.gov/publications/corfidi/sunset.html",
      "title": "The Science of Sunsets - Storm Prediction Center - NOAA",
      "quote": "**Figure 1**\n\nBecause of the substantial difference in the path length of sunlight between midday and sunrise or sunset, it can be said that sunrises ..."
    }
  ],
